{"answerText":"For Subarea 1, self-service storage facilities are subject to the as-of-right provisions of Section 42-19.

For Subarea 2, self-service storage facilities require a City Planning Commission special permit under Section 74-192.

No site, property, or parcel conclusion can be made without its address or BBL and the applicable official Appendix J map.","supportedPointUpserts":[],"supportedPointRemovals":[],"citationUpserts":[],"citationRemovals":[],"missingFactsAdd":[],"missingFactsRemove":[],"evidenceLimitationsAdd":[],"evidenceLimitationsRemove":[],"additionalEvidenceNeededAdd":[],"additionalEvidenceNeededRemove":[]}

{"answerText":"**The proposed FAR is 4.80.** On a 10,000-square-foot R7A zoning lot, the Section 23-22 table sets a maximum of **4.00 FAR (40,000 square feet)** for standard residences and **5.01 FAR (50,100 square feet)** for zoning lots containing qualifying affordable housing or qualifying senior housing (ZR § 23-22). Thus, 48,000 square feet exceeds the standard-residence limit by 8,000 square feet but is 2,100 square feet below the table’s 5.01-FAR ceiling.

Calculation: 48,000 sf ÷ 10,000 sf = **4.80 FAR**; 10,000 sf × 4.00 = **40,000 sf**; 10,000 sf × 5.01 = **50,100 sf**.

The description alone does not establish eligibility for the higher table column. “Qualifying affordable housing” must be an MIH development in an MIH area, a UAP development, or a building subject to an affordable housing regulatory agreement (ZR § 12-10). For a UAP development, residential FAR may exceed the standard FAR only by the amount of affordable housing provided on the UAP zoning lot, or—within a UAP Offsite Option Area—on a qualifying UAP site under ZR § 27-16(a) (ZR § 27-111). The affordable-floor-area amount must therefore be determined: if all relevant units (other than a super’s unit) are affordable, all residential floor area is affordable floor area; if any are not, affordable floor area is the affordable-unit residential area plus the prescribed proportional share of eligible common-area floor area (ZR § 27-111). Affordable housing on an MIH or UAP site is also subject to § 27-16 for the regulatory period, except housing restricted by an affordable housing regulatory agreement need comply only with § 27-16(a) and (j).","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"R7A table limits and calculation","explanation":"ZR § 23-22 assigns R7A a 4.00 maximum FAR for standard residences and a 5.01 maximum FAR for zoning lots containing qualifying affordable housing or qualifying senior housing. The stated 48,000 square feet on a 10,000-square-foot lot is 4.80 FAR. This equals 40,000 square feet at 4.00 FAR and 50,100 square feet at 5.01 FAR; the proposal is above the standard ceiling and below the qualifying-housing table ceiling.","sectionID":"20018017","sourceIDs":["6e425039-d5ae-4ce0-b4f1-bd1e156aece6"]},{"heading":"Qualification for higher column","explanation":"Qualifying affordable housing includes an MIH development in a Mandatory Inclusionary Housing area, a UAP development, or a building subject to an affordable housing regulatory agreement. A UAP development may exceed the § 23-22 standard-residence FAR only by the amount of affordable housing provided on the UAP zoning lot, or, in a UAP Offsite Option Area, on a UAP site under § 27-16(a).","sectionID":"20018523","sourceIDs":["56f2a76f-1c50-4499-946f-5e3478cf9a39","b5b53e7e-e014-414a-b9f7-2f35724f2d54"]},{"heading":"Affordable-floor-area determination and site requirements","explanation":"For an MIH or UAP site with all applicable units affordable other than a super’s unit, all residential floor area is affordable floor area. If one or more units are not affordable, affordable floor area is affordable-unit residential floor area plus the prescribed proportional share of eligible common-area floor area. Affordable housing in an MIH or UAP site must meet § 27-16 for the regulatory period, except housing restricted by an affordable housing regulatory agreement need comply only with § 27-16(a) and (j).","sectionID":"20022711","sourceIDs":["b5b53e7e-e014-414a-b9f7-2f35724f2d54","e050dd6f-9858-4498-9808-fb54e3a6d42d"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["Which qualifying route is asserted: MIH development in an MIH area, UAP development, or a building subject to an affordable housing regulatory agreement.","For an MIH or UAP route, the affordable-unit residential floor area; any non-affordable dwelling or rooming-unit residential floor area other than a super’s unit; and eligible common-area residential floor area, so affordable floor area can be calculated.","For a UAP route, whether the affordable housing is on the UAP zoning lot or, if offsite, whether the project is within a UAP Offsite Option Area and the UAP-site location satisfies § 27-16(a).","For an affordable-housing-regulatory-agreement route, the executed agreement and its qualifying basis, including its required affordability restrictions and public-funding connection."],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["This answer is limited to the supplied current continuously amended Zoning Resolution passages; it does not establish mapped MIH-area, UAP Offsite Option Area, or other location-based applicability."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["Project affordability documents and floor-area schedule sufficient to establish the qualifying route and calculate affordable floor area.","If applicable, MIH/UAP location and regulatory-period compliance documentation, including the applicable § 27-16 conditions. "],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"20018017","sourceIDs":["6e425039-d5ae-4ce0-b4f1-bd1e156aece6"],"relevance":"R7A table values and resulting standard and qualifying-housing floor-area ceilings."},{"sectionID":"20018523","sourceIDs":["56f2a76f-1c50-4499-946f-5e3478cf9a39"],"relevance":"Definition of FAR and qualifying affordable housing."},{"sectionID":"20022699","sourceIDs":["b5b53e7e-e014-414a-b9f7-2f35724f2d54"],"relevance":"Affordable-floor-area calculation and UAP FAR limitation."},{"sectionID":"20022711","sourceIDs":["e050dd6f-9858-4498-9808-fb54e3a6d42d"],"relevance":"MIH/UAP-site requirements and limited regulatory-agreement exception."}]}

{"answerText":"No—on the stated facts, the 5,000-square-foot storage level does not count as zoning floor area, provided that the stated base-plane measurement remains controlling and the special lowered-yard rule does not reclassify any portion. ZR § 12-10 defines a cellar, where a base plane is used to determine building height, as a space wholly or partly below the base plane with more than one-half of its floor-to-ceiling height below the base plane. Because the level is a cellar and is not used for dwelling purposes, it is excluded from floor area; the 5,000-square-foot size does not change that result.

That conclusion remains conditional because the supplied facts do not establish the special measurement conditions. If a sloping base plane is established, the cellar test is measured from the street-wall-line level used to establish that base plane. The supplied facts also do not establish whether a through-lot street-wall-line measurement is relevant. Most importantly, it is unknown whether, after December 5, 1990, any yard on the zoning lot—other than the specified garage-entrance portion—was lowered below the base-plane level. Under that condition, floor space with at least one-half of its height above curb level is considered a basement, which could affect the classification.

The separate retail-cellar rule is limited to calculations for accessory off-street parking, accessory bicycle parking, and accessory off-street loading; it does not make the stated storage level general zoning floor area.","supportedPointUpserts":[{"targetIndex":0,"value":{"heading":"Cellar exclusion from floor area","explanation":"ZR § 12-10 defines a cellar, where a base plane is used, as a space wholly or partly below the base plane with more than one-half of its height (measured from floor to ceiling) below the base plane. On the stated facts, the storage level is a cellar and is not used for dwelling purposes; accordingly, the non-dwelling cellar exclusion applies and the 5,000 square feet is excluded from zoning floor area, subject to the unresolved special measurement conditions.","sectionID":"20018523","sourceIDs":["4a7bd60e-008c-4de3-acb8-eadeaf073a36"]}},{"targetIndex":1,"value":{"heading":"Special measurement qualifications","explanation":"The supplied ZR § 12-10 passage provides that, when a sloping base plane is established, cellar height is measured from the street-wall-line level used to establish that base plane. It also provides that, where a yard was lowered below the base-plane level after December 5, 1990 (except the specified garage-entrance portion), floor space with at least one-half of its height above curb level is considered a basement. Whether those conditions apply is unresolved. Whether a through-lot street-wall-line measurement is relevant is also unresolved on the supplied facts.","sectionID":"20018523","sourceIDs":["4a7bd60e-008c-4de3-acb8-eadeaf073a36"]}}],"supportedPointRemovals":[],"citationUpserts":[{"targetIndex":0,"value":{"sectionID":"20018523","sourceIDs":["4a7bd60e-008c-4de3-acb8-eadeaf073a36"],"relevance":"ZR § 12-10 supplies the controlling base-plane cellar definition and the sloping-base-plane and post-December 5, 1990 lowered-yard measurement conditions."}}],"citationRemovals":[],"missingFactsAdd":["Whether, subsequent to December 5, 1990, any yard on the zoning lot—other than the specified portion in front of a garage entrance—was lowered below the applicable base-plane level; if so, the supplied definition treats floor space with at least one-half of its height above curb level as a basement."],"missingFactsRemove":[],"evidenceLimitationsAdd":[],"evidenceLimitationsRemove":[],"additionalEvidenceNeededAdd":[],"additionalEvidenceNeededRemove":[]}
